<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> camber bends up in the middle, giving strong edge grip, pop, and stability on hard snow. Rocker bends down in the middle (tips up), making boards and skis looser, easier to turn, and better in powder. Hybrids combine both, and they're the best choice for most riders who want one board or pair of skis for everything.</p></div>

<h2>What the terms mean</h2>
<p>Lay a snowboard or ski on a flat floor and look at it from the side. The shape you see is its <strong>profile</strong>, and it changes how the board grips, turns, and floats.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Camber:</strong> the middle arches up off the floor while the contact points near the tips and tails touch down. When you stand on it, your weight presses the arch flat, loading the edges along their full length.</li>
  <li><strong>Rocker (reverse camber):</strong> the middle touches the floor and the tips and tails curve up, like the runners of a rocking chair.</li>
  <li><strong>Flat:</strong> the base lies flat between the contact points, a middle ground between the two.</li>
  <li><strong>Hybrid:</strong> combinations, such as camber underfoot with rocker in the tips, or rocker between the feet with camber toward the tips.</li>
</ul>

