<h2>Camber: grip and pop</h2>
<p>Traditional camber puts pressure on the edges from tip to tail, so it holds a carve on firm snow and feels stable at speed. The arch also stores energy like a spring, which gives a lively "pop" off jumps and rollers. The downside is that it's less forgiving: if an edge catches, it grabs. Camber suits confident riders who spend most of their time on groomed runs, carving, or hitting jumps.</p>

<h2>Rocker: easy turns and float</h2>
<p>With the tips lifted, a rockered board or ski pivots easily and is much less likely to catch an edge, which makes it friendly for learning. In deep snow, the upturned tips rise to the surface instead of diving, so you float with less effort. The trade-off is less grip and stability on hard or icy snow and a looser, "surfy" feel at speed.</p>

<h2>Hybrid profiles: the best of both</h2>
<p>Most modern boards and skis use a hybrid profile, and for good reason. Two common versions:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Camber underfoot, rocker in the tips:</strong> keeps edge hold and pop where your weight is while adding float and forgiveness at the ends. Very common in all-mountain skis and boards.</li>
  <li><strong>Rocker between the feet, camber toward the tips (snowboards):</strong> easy to turn and playful, with extra grip near the contact points.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How profile affects skis vs snowboards</h2>
<p>The same principles apply to both, but skis describe rocker mostly at the tip and tail. Skis with lots of tip rocker have less edge touching the snow, so they ski shorter than their measured length. That's why powder skis are often sized a bit longer. The <%= link_to "ski size calculator", ski_size_chart_path %> adds length for powder for this reason, and the <%= link_to "snowboard size calculator", snowboard_size_chart_path %> does the same for powder boards.</p>

<h2>Which should you choose?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Beginner:</strong> rocker or a rocker-dominant hybrid. You'll fall less and progress faster.</li>
  <li><strong>Intermediate, all-mountain:</strong> a hybrid with camber underfoot. It handles groomers, some powder, and the occasional jump.</li>
  <li><strong>Carving and hard snow:</strong> traditional camber or camber-dominant hybrid.</li>
  <li><strong>Powder days:</strong> generous tip rocker, or a dedicated powder shape.</li>
</ul>
<p>For more on picking a board or skis, see <%= link_to "how to choose a snowboard", guide_path("best-snowboard") %> and <%= link_to "how to choose all-mountain skis", guide_path("best-all-mountain-skis") %>.</p>
<% faqs << { question: "Is camber or rocker better for beginners?", answer: "Rocker, or a rocker-dominant hybrid. The lifted tips make turning easier and reduce the chance of catching an edge." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Is camber or rocker better for powder?", answer: "Rocker. Upturned tips stay on top of deep snow instead of diving, so you float with less effort." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "What is a hybrid camber profile?", answer: "A mix of profiles in one board or ski, most often camber underfoot for grip and pop with rocker in the tips for easier turns and float. Hybrids are the most common all-mountain choice." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Does rocker change what size I need?", answer: "Often, yes. Rocker reduces the length of edge in contact with the snow, so heavily rockered skis and powder boards are usually sized a little longer." } %>
